    "created_at": "2021-07-12T21:27:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-07-12T21:27:31Z",
    "body": "I'm hesitant to do this, because I've seen the results in Bikeshed-using specs: one is never sure which ID to link to, and gets inconsistent results. See e.g. https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-permissions-policy/#algo-construct-policy vs. construct-policy-from-dictionary-and-origin which is referenced in two ways: one in https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-permissions-policy/#algo-process-response-policy and one in https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-permissions-policy/#permissions-policy-http-header-field .\r\n\r\nI'd much rather Bikeshed/WebRef move toward a model like HTML has, where only one dfn is allowed inside a heading, and cross-links to that dfn instead get re-targeted at the heading. Failing that, it would probably be a good idea to keep the special-case in WebRef for HTML.\r\n\r\nWe could also sidestep the problem by moving all `<dfn>`s out of heading elements, which feels natural in some cases. But especially for HTML element definitions, it feels less natural, and I like the current structure where a link to the definition of `small` puts you at https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/text-level-semantics.html#the-small-element.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
